WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.620
For the past decade or more, the Marvel Cinematic
Universe has dominated box the box office

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.280
and pop culture as a whole.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:11.620
But sandwiched in between the MCU mega-blockbusters
Captain Marvel and Avengers: Endgame, there's

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:17.080
another little superhero movie from the underdogs
at DC Comics, and it's actually really awesome.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:17.960
How awesome?

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.620
Well, here’s a spoiler-filled look at the
four best and four worst things about Shazam!

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.610
The films of the DC Extended Universe have
a well-earned reputation for being dark, grim,

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.400
and gritty, which a lot of audiences felt
weren't too much fun to watch.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:36.250
There’s a lot of angst and moral ambivalence
and very little in the way of actual heroism.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.520
Instead, they seem to feature angry people
who clearly aren’t getting enough fiber

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.300
in their diets.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:46.140
So maybe the biggest surprise about Shazam!
is that it’s not just funny, it’s also

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.260
actually fun from start to finish.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:53.340
Director David F. Sandberg mostly does a great
job keeping the tone upbeat and energetic

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.740
as teen Billy Batson discovers his incredible
new powers.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.780
It’s true that Aquaman also dared to have
at least a good time along the way, but Billy’s

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.470
exuberance at becoming a superhero is something
we haven’t really seen before, and is a

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.620
welcome change compared to the DCEU’s usual
dark and violent fare.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.060
"Did you believe?"

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.020
"...yeah!"

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.120
On the other hand… while Shazam! is mostly
family friendly fun, once in awhile it just

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.300
sort of forgets itself and veers off into
the realm of horror.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.400
Of course, this makes some sense considering
that Sandberg is best known for directing

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.420
the acclaimed horror hits Lights Out and Annabelle:
Creation.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.880
Still, the tonal shifts are jarring.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.760
One minute, Billy and his foster brother Freddy
Freeman are clowning around doing the floss,

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:44.070
and then the next minute, the evil Dr. Sivana
is summoning a horde of demons to literally

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.200
eat people’s heads off.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.680
"Oh, snap!

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:48.920
You're like a bad guy, right?"

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.120
These abrupt detours into terror feel kind
of out of place and unnecessary.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.580
Luckily, it doesn’t happen often, but even
a couple of times might be a couple of times

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.200
too many.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:02.280
Across the board, the stars of Shazam! deliver
and then some.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.990
Zachary Levi, as the superheroic adult form
that young Billy takes, maintains a level

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.300
of boyish wonder while using his grown-up
physicality to great comedic effect.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.319
He also has terrific rapport with Jack Dylan
Grazer as Billy’s foster brother Freddy

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:16.960
Freeman.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.140
And Grazer similarly has excellent chemistry
with Asher Angel, who plays the younger, less-mighty

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.380
mortal version of Billy.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.359
It’s hard enough for most movies to find
even one star that’s right for any given

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:27.860
part.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:32.159
But Shazam! somehow nailed the casting for
its three biggest roles, and the movie is

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.420
much better for it.

00:02:33.689 --> 00:02:38.140
Still, not every role comes off as well as
it could have, and one area where the movie

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.580
really falls short is its villains.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.320
That’s not actually a knock on actor Mark
Strong, who plays the lead villain, Dr. Thaddeus

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:45.820
Sivana.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.940
Strong has a long history of playing baddies
in films, and he’s very good at being very

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:51.440
bad.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.920
But unfortunately, while all the stuff with
Billy and Freddy feels fresh and new, the

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:00.000
scenes with Dr. Sivana feel like they were
cobbled together from a list of boring super

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.000
villain cliches.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.560
Plus, his demonic CGI helpers have boring
designs.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.560
The coolest thing Shazam! did with villains
was teasing Mr. Mind, an evil genius worm,

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.700
in the mid-credits scene.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.760
We just wish he had been the lead villain
of this one too.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.480
At least that wouldn’t have been just more
of the same.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.549
When moviemakers don’t respect the comics
their films are based on, the result is always

00:03:21.549 --> 00:03:22.780
bad, and always disappointing.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.499
Just look at films like Jonah Hex, or even
Man of Steel, which was controversial among

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.300
longtime comic book fans.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.360
In that one, many have said that director
Zack Snyder didn’t believe the concept of

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.900
a pure hero like Superman was relevant.

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:40.140
The impulse to make comic book movies "more
serious" because the filmmakers want to be

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.360
taken seriously is never a good thing.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:47.120
Which is why it’s so great that Shazam!
actually revels in its comic book origins.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.480
The filmmakers don’t mind being goofy, because
they know it's fine as long as audiences are

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:52.760
brought along for the ride.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.260
Characters like Mr. Mind are silly but they're
exactly the kind of silly we’ve been waiting

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:58.320
for all along.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:03:59.680
Shazam!

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.669
Is trying to do a lot of different things.

00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:05.659
It’s got comedy, it’s got action, it’s
got a heart-warming family message at its

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.169
gooey center, and it’s a modern superhero
fable.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.740
But while it does most of those things pretty
well, trying to do them all at once results

00:04:12.749 --> 00:04:17.250
is a movie-going experience that's kind of
like trying to eat a Chipotle burrito: it’s

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:17.980
just overstuffed.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.240
And that over-ambition shows in the film’s
run time.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:25.700
For a fast-paced, fun movie, Shazam! isn’t
actually all that fast-paced.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.240
Instead, it runs a whopping 2 hours and 12
minutes, which is maybe a half hour too long.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:36.620
Shazam! is really good, but like the old saying
goes, sometimes you can get too much of a

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:37.300
good thing.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.880
A little restraint would have been better
for the movie… and better for your bladder.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.060
"Superpowers?

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.740
Dude, I don't even know how to PEE in this
thing!"

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:48.440
Like all good films, Shazam! is actually about
something more than simple superhero fist

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:48.940
fights.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.760
At the heart of the film is young Billy Batson
and his quest to find his family.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:57.400
In the end, he realizes his true family was
right in front of him all along.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.160
It’s a great arc, made all the better by
the performances of the actors who play his

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.760
foster siblings, especially Faith Herman as
the precocious Darla.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:09.380
Even better, the twist ending where all his
siblings are granted the powers of the wizard

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.680
Shazam is a great callback to the comics.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:16.160
Seeing Billy and his siblings come together
to fight crime was a great surprise that delivered

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.940
both the action and the feels at the same
time.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.960
About the only thing that could have made
the superhero family twist even better was

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:25.020
if we hadn’t already known it was coming.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:27.130
So who spoiled it for everyone?

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.080
No, it wasn’t some paparazzi or gossip website.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.770
Instead, it’s the modern scourge of film
fans everywhere: merchandise tie-ins.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.500
Yes, unfortunately images of the action figures
were leaked months before the film’s April

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.700
release, spoiling the surprise.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.860
Hey, toy companies, next time could you please
keep it zipped until after the movie is out?

